    "thm-dpo-reward-policy-reparameterization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-bradley-terry-preference-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a finite nonempty prompt set. For each $x\\in X$, let $Y_x$ be a finite response set with at least two elements, and let $r_x:Y_x\\to\\mathbb R$ be a real-valued reward. The Bradley–Terry model assigns to each ordered pair of distinct responses $a,b\\in Y_x$ the two-outcome comparison $a\\succ b$ or $b\\succ a$, with\n$$\\Pr(a\\succ b\\mid x)=\\sigma\\bigl(r_x(a)-r_x(b)\\bigr),\\qquad \\sigma(t)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-t}}.$$\nHere $a\\succ b$ means that response $a$ is preferred to response $b$ at prompt $x$; the probability of the reverse outcome is $1-\\Pr(a\\succ b\\mid x)=\\sigma(r_x(b)-r_x(a))$. This is a statistical model for pairwise comparisons, not a universal law of human preference.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-kl-regularized-policy-improvement",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $Y$ be a finite nonempty set. A probability vector on $Y$ is a family $p=(p_y)_{y\\in Y}$ with $p_y\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{y\\in Y}p_y=1$. For probability vectors $p,q$, define the finite relative entropy\n$$D(p\\|q)=\\sum_{y:p_y>0}p_y\\log\\frac{p_y}{q_y}$$\nwhen $p_y=0$ whenever $q_y=0$, and define $D(p\\|q)=+\\infty$ otherwise. Terms with $p_y=0$ are omitted. Given a real reward $r:Y\\to\\mathbb R$ and a coefficient $\\beta>0$, define\n$$F_{q,r,\\beta}(p)=\\sum_{y\\in Y}p_y r(y)-\\beta D(p\\|q).$$\nThe finite KL-regularized policy-improvement problem is to maximize $F_{q,r,\\beta}$ over all probability vectors $p$ on $Y$. The reference $q$ has full support when $q_y>0$ for every $y\\in Y$; in that case $F_{q,r,\\beta}(p)$ is finite for every $p$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-finite-action-gibbs-policy-optimum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Y$ be a finite nonempty set, let $q$ be a probability vector on $Y$ with $q_y>0$ for every $y$, let $r:Y\\to\\mathbb R$, and let $\\beta>0$. Define\n$$Z=\\sum_{y\\in Y}q_y e^{r(y)/\\beta},\\qquad p_y^*=\\frac{q_y e^{r(y)/\\beta}}{Z}.$$\nThen $Z$ is finite and positive, $p^*$ is a full-support probability vector, the maximum objective value is $F_{q,r,\\beta}(p^*)=\\beta\\log Z$, and for every probability vector $p$ on $Y$,\n$$F_{q,r,\\beta}(p^*)-F_{q,r,\\beta}(p)=\\beta D(p\\|p^*)\\ge0.$$\nEquality holds exactly when $p=p^*$. Thus $p^*$ is the unique maximizer of the finite KL-regularized policy-improvement objective.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
